namespace Ponca
{
/*!
  \page spatialpartitioning Spatial Partitioning module: user manual

  \tableofcontents

  \section spatialpartitioning_intro Introduction

  This module provides spatial data structures that accelerate neighbor queries in point clouds, typically to
  collect the neighborhoods processed by the \ref fitting. Include its header:
  \code
  #include <Ponca/SpatialPartitioning>
  \endcode

  \subsection spatialpartitioning_intro_structures Data structures

  - Ponca::KdTreeDense and Ponca::KdTreeSparse: binary space partitioning trees
    (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K-d_tree" target="_blank">k-d trees</a>). Ponca::KdTreeSparse can be
    built over a subset of the points. Both share the API of Ponca::KdTree. Kd-trees built on the CPU can be
    queried in CUDA kernels (see \ref spatialpartitioning_kdtree_cuda).
  - Ponca::KnnGraph: the graph connecting every point to its k nearest neighbors
    (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nearest_neighbor_graph" target="_blank">k-NN graph</a>), built from a
    kd-tree.
  - Ponca::NeighborGraph: the graph connecting every point to all the points closer than a given distance, built
    from a kd-tree.

  All the data structures work in arbitrary dimension.

  \subsection spatialpartitioning_intro_queries Queries

  %Ponca queries are defined by their input (the index of a point of the data structure, Ponca::QueryInputIsIndex, or
  an arbitrary position, Ponca::QueryInputIsPosition) and by their output (the nearest neighbor,
  Ponca::QueryOutputIsNearest, the k nearest neighbors, Ponca::QueryOutputIsKNearest, or the neighbors closer than a
  radius, Ponca::QueryOutputIsRange):

  | Input      | Nearest neighbor          | k nearest neighbors        | Range neighborhood       |
  |------------|---------------------------|----------------------------|--------------------------|
  | Index      | Ponca::NearestIndexQuery  | Ponca::KNearestIndexQuery  | Ponca::RangeIndexQuery   |
  | Position   | Ponca::NearestPointQuery  | Ponca::KNearestPointQuery  | Ponca::RangePointQuery   |

  These classes provide the same API for all the data structures. The queries are created by the data structure, and
  their results are exposed as ranges of indices, accessed through iterators:
  \code
  #pragma omp parallel for
  for (int i = 0; i < nbPoints; ++i)               // for all the points of the point cloud
      for (int j : kdtree.kNearestNeighbors(i, k)) // collect the k nearest neighbors of the point i
      {
          // process the neighbor of index j
      }
  \endcode

  The semantics of the results are the following:
  - the indices refer to the point container of the data structure (`points()`),
  - queries from an index never return the queried index itself,
  - k-nearest neighbors are returned sorted by increasing distance, and `k` is bounded by `Traits::MAX_KNN_SIZE`
    (128 with the default traits),
  - range queries return the points strictly closer than the radius, in no particular order.

  \note Queries are objects independent from the data structure (they only refer to it), so they can be created and
  used concurrently by several threads. They are evaluated lazily: the search is performed when iterating over them.

  Thanks to the iterator formalism, queries can be directly given to Ponca::Basket::computeWithIds:
  \code
  Fit fit;
  fit.setNeighborFilter({position, radius});
  fit.computeWithIds(kdtree.rangeNeighbors(position, radius), kdtree.points());
  \endcode

  \section spatialpartitioning_kdtree KdTree

  \subsection spatialpartitioning_kdtree_implementation Specifications

  In %Ponca, the kd-tree is a binary tree in which:
  - each inner node splits its bounding box in two halves, at the center of the box, along its largest dimension
    (the tree is therefore not balanced in general),
  - a node becomes a leaf when it contains at most `minCellSize()` samples (64 by default, see
    Ponca::StaticKdTreeBase::setMinCellSize), or when it reaches the maximal depth `Traits::MAX_DEPTH` (32 with the
    default traits),
  - points are only referenced by the leaves, through a contiguous range of the array of samples,
  - the queries perform a depth-first traversal with a fixed-size stack, and prune the nodes that are farther than the
    current search distance,
  - the initial order of the points is kept: only the array of samples is reordered.

  \subsection spatialpartitioning_kdtree_usage Basic usage

  Ponca::KdTreeDense builds a tree over all the points of a container. Ponca::KdTreeSparse provides the same
  functionalities, but can also be built from a subset of the points. Both share the API of their base class
  Ponca::KdTree.

  \subsubsection spatialpartitioning_kdtree_usage_construction Construction

  As for the other modules, the point type is a template parameter `DataPoint` (see \ref ponca_points). The points
  are copied into the tree at construction, for instance from a random point cloud:
  \snippet examples/cpp/ponca_neighbor_search.cpp Kdtree Dense construction

  The tree can also be default-constructed and built later with `build(points)`, e.g. after changing the maximal size
  of the leaves with `setMinCellSize()`. `build(points, converter)` converts a container of custom points into the
  points of the tree with a user-provided functor (see Ponca::KdTreeBase::build).

  A Ponca::KdTreeSparse can be built from a set of indices, to sample a subset of the input points. Here, to select
  half of the points randomly:
  \snippet examples/cpp/ponca_neighbor_search.cpp Kdtree Sampling construction

  \subsubsection spatialpartitioning_kdtree_usage_queries Queries

  Queries are objects generated by the kd-tree, and are designed as ranges: the neighbors are accessed by iterating
  over the query. For instance, the k nearest neighbors of the point of index `query_idx` are obtained as follows:
  \snippet examples/cpp/ponca_neighbor_search.cpp Kdtree k-nearest neighbors index search

  The kd-tree provides one function per query type, each of them taking either an index or a position:
  - `nearestNeighbor(input)`, `kNearestNeighbors(input, k)` and `rangeNeighbors(input, radius)` create and set up a
    query,
  - `nearestNeighborQuery()`, `kNearestNeighborsQuery()`, `rangeNeighborsQuery()` (position input) and
    `nearestNeighborIndexQuery()`, `kNearestNeighborsIndexQuery()`, `rangeNeighborsIndexQuery()` (index input) create
    an empty query, to be set up later.

  Queries are mutable and can be reused, by calling them with new parameters. Here, a range query is reused with a
  different point index, `second_query_idx`:
  \snippet examples/cpp/ponca_neighbor_search.cpp Kdtree range neighbors index mutable search

  An empty query can also be created, then called with its parameters:
  \snippet examples/cpp/ponca_neighbor_search.cpp Kdtree range neighbors position mutable search

  The type of the input is part of the type of the query: a position query cannot be reused with an index, a new query
  must be created.

  The available query classes are (see Ponca::KdTreeBase for the related functions):
  - Ponca::KdTreeKNearestQueryBase, specialized by Ponca::KdTreeKNearestIndexQuery and Ponca::KdTreeKNearestPointQuery,
  - Ponca::KdTreeNearestQueryBase, specialized by Ponca::KdTreeNearestIndexQuery and Ponca::KdTreeNearestPointQuery,
  - Ponca::KdTreeRangeQueryBase, specialized by Ponca::KdTreeRangeIndexQuery and Ponca::KdTreeRangePointQuery.

  These queries are illustrated in \ref example_cxx_neighbor_search, and used in the tests
  (`tests/src/queries_knearest.cpp`, `tests/src/queries_nearest.cpp`, `tests/src/queries_range.cpp`,
  `tests/src/basket.cpp`) and in \ref example_cxx_nanoflann_page.

  \subsubsection spatialpartitioning_kdtree_usage_samples_and_indexing Samples and indexing

  There are two ways of iterating over a kd-tree: over its points, or over its samples. The points are the
  underlying data storage, which the kd-tree does not reorder, while the samples are an array of indices into the
  point storage, reordered by the kd-tree during its construction to group the points of each leaf.

  Indices into the point storage are called *point indices*, and indices into the sample storage *sample indices*.
  Note that the sample storage contains point indices: the sample number 0 may, for example, refer to the point
  number 25. The point storage is accessed with Ponca::StaticKdTreeBase::points, and the array of samples with
  Ponca::StaticKdTreeBase::samples. For a Ponca::KdTreeSparse, `sampleCount()` is smaller than `pointCount()`.

  The API of the kd-tree takes and returns point indices. To iterate over the samples instead (e.g. with a
  Ponca::KdTreeSparse), convert sample indices to point indices with Ponca::StaticKdTreeBase::pointFromSample (see
  also Ponca::StaticKdTreeBase::pointDataFromSample).

  \subsection spatialpartitioning_kdtree_usage_which_class Usage of the classes KdTree and KdTreeBase

  Ponca::KdTree is the common interface of Ponca::KdTreeDense and Ponca::KdTreeSparse. It can be used to store or
  pass references or pointers to either of them:
  \snippet examples/cpp/ponca_neighbor_search.cpp KdTree pointer usage
  \snippet examples/cpp/ponca_neighbor_search.cpp KdTree assign sparse
  \snippet examples/cpp/ponca_neighbor_search.cpp KdTree assign dense

  Ponca::KdTreeBase, and its base class Ponca::StaticKdTreeBase, provide these abstractions for arbitrary traits
  (buildable and read-only trees, respectively). When the type of `Traits` is arbitrary (but known at compile time),
  use one of these classes to declare function parameters or variables. In the class Ponca::KdTreeQuery, for
  instance, the link to the kd-tree is stored as follows:
  \snippet kdTreeQuery.h KdTreeQuery kdtree type
  Here, `m_kdtree` can be either dense or sparse, and have any type of `Traits`.

  \subsection spatialpartitioning_kdtree_extending Customizing the KdTree using Traits

  The containers and the node types of the trees are defined by a `Traits` class. Ponca::KdTreeDefaultTraits provides
  general-purpose traits that fit most usages, and is used by Ponca::KdTree, Ponca::KdTreeDense and
  Ponca::KdTreeSparse:
  \snippet kdTree.h KdTree type definition
  \snippet kdTree.h KdTreeDense type definition
  \snippet kdTree.h KdTreeSparse type definition

  Custom traits can change the containers, the index types and the maximal depth, or store additional data in the
  nodes. For instance, `examples/cpp/ponca_customize_kdtree.cpp` stores the bounding box of the inner nodes:
  - define an inner node type storing the box,
    \snippet ponca_customize_kdtree.cpp CustomInnerNodeDefinition
  - define a node type using it (see Ponca::KdTreeCustomizableNode), and fill the new attribute when the node is
    configured,
    \snippet ponca_customize_kdtree.cpp CustomNodeDefinition
  - define a kd-tree type using this node type,
    \snippet ponca_customize_kdtree.cpp KdTreeTypeWithCustomNode
  - read the new attribute from the nodes of the tree.
    \snippet ponca_customize_kdtree.cpp ReadCustomProperties

  See \ref example_cxx_customize_kdtree_page for the complete program.

  \subsection spatialpartitioning_kdtree_cuda Usage in CUDA kernels

  Kd-trees are built on the CPU. Their buffers (points, nodes and samples) can then be copied to the GPU and wrapped
  in a read-only tree (Ponca::StaticKdTreeBase) whose traits use raw pointers (Ponca::KdTreePointerTraits), which can
  be queried in kernels. This is detailed in \ref ponca_gpu_page.

  \section spatialpartitioning_knngraph KnnGraph

  \subsection spatialpartitioning_knngraph_usage Basic usage

  Ponca::KnnGraph stores, for every point, the indices of its k nearest neighbors. Neighbor queries then only read the
  graph instead of traversing a tree.

  \subsubsection spatialpartitioning_knngraph_usage_construction Construction

  The graph is constructed from a kd-tree, and accesses the points of the kd-tree without copying them:
  \snippet examples/cpp/ponca_neighbor_search.cpp KnnGraph construction

  \warning The kd-tree must index all its points (no sub-sampling), and must outlive the graph. `k` is reduced to
  `sampleCount() - 1` when it is larger.

  \subsubsection spatialpartitioning_knngraph_usage_queries Queries

  As for kd-trees, queries are objects designed as ranges. The neighbors stored in the graph are accessed with
  `oneConnectedNeighbors(index)` (or `kNearestNeighbors(index)`):
  \snippet examples/cpp/ponca_neighbor_search.cpp KnnGraph k-nearest neighbors index search

  \note Graphs can only be queried from the index of one of their points.

  Range queries are also available. Starting from the queried point, they traverse the graph and collect the points
  closer than the radius: only the points connected to the query through a path of points in the ball are returned.
  This differs from a kd-tree range query when the k-NN graph does not connect the whole ball (e.g. when the point
  cloud has several components, or when `k` is small with respect to the radius). Queries are mutable and can be
  reused:
  \snippet examples/cpp/ponca_neighbor_search.cpp KnnGraph range neighbors index mutable search

  Two types of queries are provided (see Ponca::AbstractNeighborGraph for the related functions):
  - Ponca::NeighborGraphOneConnectedQuery: the neighbors stored in the graph. For a k-NN graph, the number of
    neighbors is defined at construction: the graph only provides k-neighborhoods. A nearest neighbor query is the
    first element of this query when the graph is built with `k = 1`;
  - Ponca::NeighborGraphRangeQuery: the range query described above.

  \section spatialpartitioning_neighborgraph NeighborGraph

  Ponca::NeighborGraph generalizes the k-NN graph: it connects every point to all the points closer than a given
  distance, so that every point has its own number of neighbors. It is constructed from a kd-tree and the connection
  distance (`NeighborGraph<DataPoint> graph(kdtree, distance)`), with the same restrictions as Ponca::KnnGraph, and
  provides the same queries.
 */
}
